<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Example Plays</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A1.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="2566.84" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 653.15 2566.84" width="653.15"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,2566.84) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#B3B3B3;--ltx-fill-color:#B3B3B3;--ltx-fg-color:#B3B3B3;" color="#B3B3B3" fill="#B3B3B3" fill-opacity="0.5" stroke="#B3B3B3" stroke-opacity="0.5"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 3.54 4.87 L 3.54 2554.89 C 3.54 2559.53 7.31 2563.3 11.96 2563.3 L 648.28 2563.3 C 652.93 2563.3 656.69 2559.53 656.69 2554.89 L 656.69 4.87 C 656.69 0.22 652.93 -3.54 648.28 -3.54 L 11.96 -3.54 C 7.31 -3.54 3.54 0.22 3.54 4.87 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#B3D9FF;" fill="#B3D9FF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 6.64 L 0 2560.2 C 0 2563.87 2.97 2566.84 6.64 2566.84 L 646.51 2566.84 C 650.18 2566.84 653.15 2563.87 653.15 2560.2 L 653.15 6.64 C 653.15 2.97 650.18 0 646.51 0 L 6.64 0 C 2.97 0 0 2.97 0 6.64 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FCFEFF;" fill="#FCFEFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.11 6.64 L 1.11 2544.46 L 652.04 2544.46 L 652.04 6.64 C 652.04 3.58 649.56 1.11 646.51 1.11 L 6.64 1.11 C 3.58 1.11 1.11 3.58 1.11 6.64 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.11 2552.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:39.02em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="539.93"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:39.02em;">
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Box 4: Challenger-Generated Prompts and Solver-Generated Responses</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 16.11 2506)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:44.87em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:180.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="2518.9" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="620.87"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:44.87em;">
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">500 iterations.
<br class="ltx_break"></span><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF7D00;">What is the typical difference in approach between a Montessori classroom and a traditional classroom setup when it comes to student-led project-based learning?<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;"> In a Montessori classroom, student-led project-based learning is a core approach to education, whereas in traditional classrooms, it’s often teacher-led. Here are the main differences:</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Montessori Approach<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">:</span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">1. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Self-Directed Learning</span>: Montessori students are encouraged to take ownership of their learning process, making choices about topics, methods, and pace. 
<br class="ltx_break">2. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Project-Based Learning</span>: Students work on self-chosen projects that align with their interests and learning objectives, promoting in-depth exploration and mastery.
<br class="ltx_break">3. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collaboration</span>: Students work in small groups, promoting peer-to-peer learning, discussion, and mutual respect.
<br class="ltx_break">4. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flexible Time</span>: Students have the freedom to work at their own pace, with minimal supervision, allowing them to take charge of their time management.
<br class="ltx_break">5. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emphasis on Process over Product</span>: The focus is on the learning process, not just the end product, encouraging students to reflect on their progress and adjust their approach as needed.

<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Traditional Classroom Approach:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">1. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teacher-Led Instruction</span>: The teacher plans and delivers the lesson, setting the agenda, pace, and content for the students.
<br class="ltx_break">2. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Project-Based Learning with Teacher Guidance</span>: Teachers assign projects, provide guidance, and facilitate discussions, but students may have limited autonomy in their choices.
<br class="ltx_break">3. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured Time</span>: Students follow a set schedule, with teachers monitoring their progress and providing direct instruction when needed.
<br class="ltx_break">4. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emphasis on Assessment</span>: The focus is on evaluating student performance and understanding through standardized tests, quizzes, and assessments.
<br class="ltx_break">5. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linear Approach</span>: The learning process is typically structured and linear, with students progressing through a set curriculum.

<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Key differences between the two approaches:</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autonomy</span>: Montessori students have more control over their learning, while traditional classrooms have a more structured environment.
<br class="ltx_break">* <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Flexibility</span>: Montessori students can adjust their pace and approach, whereas traditional classrooms have a more rigid structure.
<br class="ltx_break">* <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emphasis</span>: Montessori emphasizes the process, while traditional classrooms focus on the end product.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">While both approaches have their strengths and weaknesses, Montessori’s student-led approach is designed to foster independence, creativity, and a love of learning, whereas traditional classrooms prioritize teacher-led instruction and standardized evaluation.<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">1000 iterations.<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF7D00;">Follow these steps to build a functional submarine that can dive to a depth of 100 meters underwater while using only a scuba tank that holds 12 liters of air. The submarine must be able to withstand water pressure at that depth without collapsing, and it should surface automatically after 20 minutes.</span>

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Building a functional submarine that can dive to 100 meters while using a 12-liter scuba tank is highly challenging, if not impossible, with current technology. However, I can provide a hypothetical design that could potentially meet your requirements. Please note that this design is purely theoretical, and actual construction and testing would require extensive expertise and resources.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.9.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Design Considerations</span></span></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">1. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pressure Hull:</span> The submarine’s pressure hull must be strong enough to withstand water pressure at 100 meters. The pressure at this depth is approximately 1000 times greater than at sea level. We’ll use a cylindrical design with a diameter of 1 meter and a length of 5 meters.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">2. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Material:</span> We’ll use high-strength steel for the pressure hull, such as HY-100 or similar materials.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">3. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scuba Tank:</span> We’ll use the 12-liter scuba tank as the primary air supply.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">4. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Power Source:</span> We’ll use electric motors powered by rechargeable batteries.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">5. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Control Systems:</span> We’ll use a simple control system with buttons and a display to control the descent, ascent, and surface.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">6. <span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.15.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Life Support System:</span> We’ll include a basic life support system, but it won’t be sufficient for an extended dive.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.16.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Hypothetical Design:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.16.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.17" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.17.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Here’s a simplified design:</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.18" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Pressure Hull:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.18.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.19" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.19.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* Material: High-strength steel (1 meter diameter x 5 meters long)
<br class="ltx_break">* Thickness: 6 mm
<br class="ltx_break">* Weight: approximately 200 kg</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.20" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.20.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Scuba Tank:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.20.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.21" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.21.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* 12 liters of compressed air
<br class="ltx_break">* Tank pressure: 200 bar (approx.)
<br class="ltx_break">* Valve system to regulate air flow</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.22" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.22.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Power Source:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.22.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.23" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.23.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* 2 x rechargeable lithium-ion batteries (12V, 10 Ah)
<br class="ltx_break">* Electric motors (2 x DC, 10 kW each)
<br class="ltx_break">* Power electronics and control system</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.24" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.24.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Control Systems:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.24.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.25" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.25.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* Simple control panel with buttons for descent, ascent, and surface
<br class="ltx_break">* Display showing depth, pressure, and battery level</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.26" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.26.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Life Support System:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.26.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.27" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.27.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* Basic oxygen generator (not sufficient for extended dives)
<br class="ltx_break">* Carbon dioxide scrubber (not sufficient for extended dives)</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.28" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.28.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Autonomous Ascent:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.28.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.29" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.29.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* The submarine will use a pressure hull’s buoyancy to ascend when the air supply is depleted or when the time limit is reached.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.30" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.30.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">Calculations:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.30.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.31" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.31.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0F75FF;">* Weight of the pressure hull: 200 kg
<br class="ltx_break">* Volume of scuba tank: 12 liters
<br class="ltx_break">* Depth to reach: 100 meters<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.31.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">

<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span></span>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.32" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.32.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">1500 iterations.<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.32.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.32.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF7D00;">Follow the instructions to create a new programming language that is a combination of Python and Haskell, with a unique syntax and semantics, and can compile and run on a 64-bit x86 processor, using only the standard library and without any external dependencies, in under 1000 characters.</span></span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.33" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.33.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">I must point out that creating a full-fledged programming language in under 1000 characters is extremely challenging, if not impossible. However, I can provide a minimal example of a language that combines elements of Python and Haskell, along with a basic compiler, within the given constraints.</span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.34" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.34.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Language:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.34.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ”Pyhaskell” (yes, it’s a real name now)</span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.35" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.35.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">Syntax:<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Inspired by Python’s concise syntax and Haskell’s type inference.</span></span></span>
<span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.36" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.36.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0080FF;">“‘pyhaskell 
<br class="ltx_break">type Val = Int — String — None

<br class="ltx_break">expr = Expr 
<br class="ltx_break">expr = Lit Val of Val — App Expr Expr — Var Str 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">exprLit = Lit Val of Val
<br class="ltx_break">exprApp = App Expr Expr
<br class="ltx_break">exprVar = Var Str
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">app = App exprLit exprLit
<br class="ltx_break">“‘

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.36.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compiler:</span>

<br class="ltx_break">“‘pyhaskell
<br class="ltx_break">import re
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">def compile(expr):
<br class="ltx_break">if isinstance(expr, Var): return f’var expr’
<br class="ltx_break">elif isinstance(expr, exprLit): return f’lit exprLit’
<br class="ltx_break">elif isinstance(expr, exprApp): return f’app exprApp’
<br class="ltx_break">else: raise ValueError(’Invalid expression’)
<br class="ltx_break">“‘
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A1.p1.pic1.2.36.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example usage:
<br class="ltx_break"></span>“‘pyhaskell
<br class="ltx_break">result = app ’var’ (app ’lit’ ’1’)
<br class="ltx_break">print(compile(result)) # Output: app lit 1
<br class="ltx_break">“‘
<br class="ltx_break">This implementation is extremely simplified and lacks error handling, type checking, and optimization. However, it demonstrates the basic structure of Pyhaskell and its compatibility with Python’s standard library.

<br class="ltx_break">Please note that creating a functional programming language in a few hundred characters is not a feasible task, and this example is more of a proof of concept than a fully functional language.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
